We analyze a general branching population where individual lifetimes are independent and identically distributed with arbitrary distributions, and individuals give birth to new ones at random times following a Poisson process independently from each other. It's assumed that individuals experience mutations at a specified rate under an infinitely many alleles model, leading to a continuous frequency spectrum of genetic variation. By considering multiple scenarios, including different population sizes, various mutation rates, and time periods, we establish approximate distributions for the frequency spectrum by relating them to a continuous, continuous-time random variable. Notably, in one scenario, the frequency spectrum can be represented as the sum of continuously generated beta variables, resulting in an asymmetric distribution that diverges from an expected fit to Kingman's model. Instead, the frequency spectrum follows a modified version of the beta approximation with added logarithmic corrections in both time and population size parameters. Furthermore, we investigate the convergence of the frequency spectrum in two dimensions to a specific limit, and demonstrate that this convergence is both fast and exponential, prompting us to develop a simplified approximation that can be computationally useful even for small population sizes and early times. We validate these results through comparisons against simulations, and show that these approximations hold across a range of models including the Kingman model, the Deborah model, and the DCSBD model. Ultimately, we believe that these approximations can be a valuable tool in testing hypotheses about the evolution of a general branching population. 

