We've explored localized breathing modes, referred to as "nanoptera," in two precompressed systems: a weakly nonlinear woodpile chain and a diatomic chain composed of alternating masses. By applying long-wave-type forces and adjusting parameters, we achieved exponential decay in spatial patterns and identified multiple nanopteron solutions in each system. Notably, our research revealed that nanoptera in the woodpile chain exhibit a greater tendency to occur than in the diatomic chain. Additionally, we investigated the stability of nanoptera in the woodpile chain and observed that increasing the mass ratio between particles can stabilize nanoptera. Finally, we discussed how nanoptera in these precompressed systems could be used to achieve mechanical memory effects. 

\textit{Keywords: nanoptera, mechanical memory, precompressed systems, woodpile chain, diatomic chain, long-wave-type forces} 

